WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.428
A busy café calls out each drink when it's ready: flat white for Sam!

2
00:00:07.428 --> 00:00:10.169
At that moment Sam is washing his hands.

3
00:00:10.169 --> 00:00:11.677
Nobody calls it twice.

4
00:00:11.677 --> 00:00:13.116
His coffee goes cold.

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:18.939
Redis, a program that keeps its data in memory, has a tool that works like that, pub/sub.

6
00:00:18.939 --> 00:00:22.741
And one that writes every message down, like an order board.

7
00:00:22.741 --> 00:00:24.072
It's called a stream.

8
00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:27.440
Here's the board for paying shops.

9
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:33.880
Four payments arrive, and Redis gives each an ID: 1-1, 1-2, 1-3, 1-4.

10
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:40.266
Each night, workers settle each payment: they add it to what the company owes that shop.

11
00:00:40.266 --> 00:00:43.069
Two workers, w1 and w2, share the board.

12
00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:46.152
Each payment must go to exactly one of them.

13
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:50.408
And if a worker dies holding a payment, how would anybody know?

14
00:00:50.408 --> 00:00:51.637
Hold that question.

15
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:58.496
In the café, the baristas share one board, and each writes their initials next to an order they take.

16
00:00:58.496 --> 00:01:00.672
In Redis, that's a consumer group.

17
00:01:00.672 --> 00:01:02.208
Ours is called settlers.

18
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:10.571
w1 asks for two entries with a greater-than sign, which means: never handed to anyone in this group.

19
00:01:10.571 --> 00:01:12.205
It gets 1-1 and 1-2.

20
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.371
w2 asks the same way, and gets 1-3 and 1-4.

21
00:01:16.371 --> 00:01:19.603
Each entry went to one worker only.

22
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:28.323
Redis also writes down every delivery: the entry's ID, who has it, when it went out, and how many times it has been delivered.

23
00:01:28.323 --> 00:01:31.096
That's the pending entries list, the PEL.

24
00:01:31.096 --> 00:01:32.855
Our initials on the board.

25
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:36.556
Now w2 asks again, with the greater-than sign.

26
00:01:36.556 --> 00:01:37.846
What does it get?

27
00:01:37.846 --> 00:01:39.060
Pause and guess.

28
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:42.837
Nothing.

29
00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:48.884
Every entry has been handed out once, and the greater-than sign never hands one out twice.

30
00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:52.177
w2 settles its two and says so with XACK.

31
00:01:52.177 --> 00:01:54.605
Redis crosses them off the list.

32
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.974
Now w1 crashes, before its XACK.

33
00:01:57.974 --> 00:02:00.056
Are 1-1 and 1-2 lost?

34
00:02:00.056 --> 00:02:03.825
Or does Redis quietly give them to w2?

35
00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:04.619
Neither.

36
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.386
They stay on the list, under w1's name.

37
00:02:07.386 --> 00:02:08.433
Nothing is lost.

38
00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:09.481
It's only stuck.

39
00:02:09.481 --> 00:02:15.633
And the greater-than sign will never hand them out again, because they were already delivered.

40
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:21.336
In the café, a barista went on a break with two orders still marked with their initials.

41
00:02:21.336 --> 00:02:24.149
Every other barista politely walks past them.

42
00:02:24.149 --> 00:02:25.837
That's manners, not a plan.

43
00:02:26.033 --> 00:02:30.063
When w1 comes back, it reads its own pending entries first.

44
00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:35.732
It asks with the ID 0 instead of the greater-than sign, and gets back the same two.

45
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:38.352
Each one's delivery count goes to 2.

46
00:02:38.352 --> 00:02:43.392
When that read comes back empty, w1 switches back to the greater-than sign.

47
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:45.487
Notice what this means.

48
00:02:45.487 --> 00:02:49.649
Say w1 settled 1-1, then crashed before its XACK.

49
00:02:49.649 --> 00:02:52.961
After the restart it settles 1-1 again.

50
00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:59.566
This is called at-least-once delivery: every entry is settled at least once, and now and then twice.

51
00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:02.254
So the settling must be safe to run twice.

52
00:03:02.254 --> 00:03:06.797
Key it by the payment's id, and the second time finds the payment done.

53
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.024
w1 settles both and sends its XACK.

54
00:03:10.024 --> 00:03:11.579
The list is empty.

55
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.781
Careful: Kafka has consumer groups too, and they share little more than the name.

56
00:03:16.781 --> 00:03:20.962
A Kafka group gives each partition to one reader, with one bookmark.

57
00:03:20.962 --> 00:03:24.897
Order holds, and one stuck record holds up everything behind it.

58
00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:30.376
A Redis group keeps a state for every entry, and hands entries to whoever asks.

59
00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.179
A slow one holds up nobody.

60
00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:34.784
The price: entries finish in any order.

61
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.436
And the question we're holding: if w1 never comes back, someone must take its entries over.

62
00:03:40.436 --> 00:03:44.080
That's called claiming, and it's the next part of the lesson.

63
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:50.515
Here's a worker's loop, in Java and in Kotlin, with Jedis, a library for talking to Redis.

64
00:03:50.733 --> 00:03:54.586
Read new entries through the group, waiting up to two seconds.

65
00:03:54.586 --> 00:03:57.817
Settle each one, keyed by the payment, then XACK it.

66
00:03:57.817 --> 00:04:00.737
If settling fails, leave it pending on purpose.

67
00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:07.356
So, how does each payment reach exactly one worker, and how do we know when a worker dies holding one?

68
00:04:07.356 --> 00:04:13.803
The group hands each entry out once, and writes the worker's initials next to it until the worker says done.

69
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:15.516
One quick check.

70
00:04:15.516 --> 00:04:20.728
w2 reads a new entry, 1-5, and crashes before its XACK.

71
00:04:20.728 --> 00:04:25.465
Then w3 joins and asks with the greater-than sign.

72
00:04:25.465 --> 00:04:26.981
Does w3 get 1-5?

73
00:04:26.981 --> 00:04:28.497
Pause and think.

74
00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:31.960
No.

75
00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:41.164
1-5 was already delivered, so it stays pending under w2, until w2 comes back for it, or someone claims it.
